#!/usr/bin/env python3
# chadtab -- a successor to cron that works on its own, and happens to have synergy with C4 and the ghostwheel.
#
# A chadtab is a chad.tab (Corwin's routing language: chadgram/chadgram.tsv) whose sources are clocks.  A block
# starts at a SOURCE line ("*  NAME  CLOCK") and its steps follow on TRUNK lines ("|"), each one arrow:
#
#   # chadtab                              comments and blank lines are raw config, kept, never run
#   PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin           NAME=VALUE before the first block: the environment every job gets
#   RUNLEVEL=3                             this table's run-level (default 3); a block may say level=N
#   *  backup   cron:17 3 * * *  catchup   cron's five fields; "catchup": one run for times missed while down
#   |  --->  !sh backup.sh                 SINK to a command: "!" then the command line (run without a shell
#                                          on Windows and POSIX alike: split like a POSIX shell, no globbing)
#   |  % <--- @  status bytes ..note       CAPTURE: the command's last output line taken apart into fields by
#                                          the chadgram rules (split on blanks; the ".." field takes the rest)
#   |  -->>  log/backup.jsonl              APPEND the run's record (fields, exit, times) as one JSON line
#   |  ==>  c4:STEM KEY COL $status        BIND: a guarded write into a C4 registry (below); $field or text
#   *  pulse    every:15m+4m               every 15 minutes, 4 past the quarter (aligned to the epoch, UTC)
#   *  once     at:2026-11-11T09:00        one time, local wall clock
#   *  start    boot                       once each time the daemon starts
#
# GUARDED WRITES (the second writer C4 requires).  A registry STEM is laid out as cxr lays a table out:
#   STEM/r/KEY/COL holds the value (the row's reading); STEM/c/COL/VALUE lists the keys (the column's reading).
#   Each write is serialized (STEM/.lock, made with mkdir: atomic on every OS), written ahead as a chadgram
#   line in the journal STEM/j ("seq when level writer key col ..value"), then written by two writers, r and
#   c, each of which reads the other back (the reciprocal guard).  Only when both agree does the semaphore
#   STEM/s advance; until then no reader sees the write, and a disagreement puts the old value back and
#   journals FAILED.  A reader at run-level L sees a write made at level W when L <= W (lower or same order
#   functionality reads what was written above or beside it), and only up to the semaphore.
#
# usage:  chadtab check TABLE                      read the table; say each block, its clock and next three fires
#         chadtab next TABLE [--from ISO] [-n N]   the next N fire times of every block, merged, in order
#         chadtab clock CLOCK FROM [N]             the next N fires of one clock after FROM (what the tests ask)
#         chadtab run TABLE NAME                   run one block now (its steps, in order), as the daemon would
#         chadtab daemon TABLE [--state DIR] [--level L] [--once-at ISO]
#                                                  the scheduler: sleeps to the next fire, runs what is due,
#                                                  keeps STATE/last.json, STATE/runs.jsonl; --once-at runs one
#                                                  tick as if it were that time and stops (tests, dry rehearsal)
#         chadtab top TABLE [--state DIR]          the operational view (in the spirit of btop): blocks, next
#                                                  fire, last result, a strip of recent outcomes; q quits
#         chadtab status TABLE [--state DIR] --json   the same, for Emacs, the surface, or anything else
#         chadtab put STEM KEY COL VALUE [--level L] [--writer W]   one guarded write
#         chadtab get STEM KEY COL [--level L]     the value a reader at level L may see (exit 3: none)
#         chadtab verify STEM                      r and c agree everywhere, and the journal ends committed
#         chadtab enc STRING                       the cxr naming rule, applied to one string
#         chadtab startup TABLE [--write]          start the daemon at sign-in: a .cmd in the Windows Startup folder
#                                                  (no Task Scheduler), or a systemd user unit; says first, writes on --write
#         chadtab --version | --prove-red
# exit:   0 ok  1 usage  2 table or registry fault  3 nothing visible  4 a guard refused  5 lock not taken
